\section{Choose Which Kind of Sequence Crosses}

A managed string is not a pointer to the first element of a C
string. It may have several internal encodings, come from a
substring, or contain a zero byte when encoded in UTF-8. A C
function expecting a NUL-terminated string has no length with
which to distinguish an internal zero from the terminator. A
function accepting a byte span can distinguish those cases, but
does not automatically know whether the bytes are text. Give
these two conversions different names and types. If the public
wrapper accepts text for a terminator-based API, encode in the
promised encoding and reject embedded zeros; appending a zero is
necessary but not sufficient.

Contrast a display name with the contents of a configuration file.
The name uses UTF-8 with no embedded NUL and may pass as a
terminated string. The file contents may include arbitrary bytes and
need a span. In either case lengths in the native prototype might be
measured in bytes rather than Unicode scalars, UTF-16 code units,
or visible characters. For instance, the string consisting of one
e with an acute accent in its precomposed form takes two bytes in
UTF-8. A wrapper that allocates for one byte plus a terminator
will not have allocated enough for both encoded bytes and the
terminator.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lp{0.62\linewidth}}
\hline
Native parameter & Binding policy \\
\hline
Terminated name & Encode, reject internal zero, append one terminator. \\
Input span & Keep owner alive and address stable for the borrow. \\
Output span & Pass capacity, then check returned length before reading. \\
Owned result & Record release function; free even if conversion fails. \\
Retained input & Transfer native storage or lease through completion. \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\section{Match the Lifetime to the Last Native Use}

Crestani describes both copying and indirection through reference
objects in garbage-collected FFIs. Copying bytes to independent
storage avoids exposing movable heap addresses. A reference object
can preserve liveness and let runtime APIs find the current object
location; it does not authorize dereferencing a cached interior
pointer. This is a general property of precise moving collectors
\cite{jones2011garbage}. Pinning arranges address stability when the
runtime supports it but may constrain collection, and it does not
prevent ordinary mutation by another thread. The binding must
separately provide a read lease, a synchronization rule or a copy.

Choose between a synchronous borrow and retention before optimizing
for copy cost. When a library consumes bytes before returning, a
pin may last only for that call. When it enqueues work, returning
from the enqueue call merely changes who is responsible for the
next use. \Cref{fig:visual-389} shows a completion protocol in
which cancellation is a request; only the completion event certifies
that no worker will subsequently read the allocation. A compiler
cannot generally derive that protocol from a \texttt{const char *}
parameter. The const qualification says nothing about how long the
callee may use the pointer.

