\section{Read an Owned Result Without Leaking It on Decode Failure}

\begin{verbatim}
get_label(native_object):
    data := initialized_null_pointer_cell()
    size := initialized_zero_size_cell()
    with lease(native_object) as handle:
        status := raw_read_label(handle, address(data), address(size))
        if status != OK:
            # This API leaves data and size unowned on failure.
            return translate(status)
        ptr, n := read(data), read(size)
        try:
            require(ptr != null or n == 0)
            length := checked_host_length(n)
            if length == 0: return ""
            bytes := copy_exactly(ptr, length)
            return decode_utf8_strict(bytes)
        finally:
            if ptr != null: raw_free_label(handle, ptr)
\end{verbatim}

The invented API supplies its result length in bytes, excludes any
trailing terminator, and guarantees a readable extent of at least
that length on success. It may produce a non-null pointer for a
zero-length allocation, so the empty result must still follow the
deallocation rule. The caller does not use \texttt{strlen}, which
would both erase valid internal zeros and read past the extent when
there is no terminator. Incorrect UTF-8 is a domain error after the
native operation has succeeded; the native allocation remains owed
even then. This release function takes the object handle, so the
object lease covers release as well as read and copy.

When using a VM value stack, an index is not by itself a durable
reference. The embedding extract illustrates Lua's index-oriented
stack interface. If the wrapper obtains a byte pointer from a
VM API, follow that API's lifetime rules while keeping the owner
reachable. Do not equate the lifetime of a temporary index with
that of a separately retained object; stack restoration, pushes
or return to the caller can change which value an index denotes.

\section{Make ``Query Then Fill'' a Bounded Protocol}

Another library writes into caller-provided storage and allows a
query for required capacity. Between calls the required size may
change, for example because the label is mutable. Do not allocate
once and assume a successful fit. On a documented insufficient
capacity status, discard the partial data, validate the new size
and retry up to a fixed limit or under a snapshot lease; then
report a changing value rather than using an unbounded allocation
loop. Count the terminating byte if the API requires room for one.
For an implementation that returns the actual readable size, reject
success with a claimed length larger than the capacity. This check
does not undo writes by a nonconforming library; it prevents the
wrapper from performing an additional out-of-bounds read.

Ask the fixture for invalid UTF-8, an empty allocated buffer, an
empty null buffer and a label whose size grows between calls.
Track frees with the fixture allocator. For the retained-input
variant, arrange for the native worker to read after enqueue
returns and after one collection. The data must still be valid,
even when the managed source is already unreachable. Also mutate
the original managed array immediately after enqueue; if the
public operation offers snapshot semantics, the worker's reading
must see the pre-enqueue bytes. That test distinguishes a stable
address from a stable value.

\section{Handle Completion Before the Submission Call Returns}

Consider an async compressor that calls completion exactly once
on acceptance, possibly during submission, and never on rejection.
The submitting thread owns a reference to a native job object.
Create a second, provisional reference for completion before calling
the compressor. Completion records the outcome and releases its
reference; it cannot destroy the job while the submitter still
owns the other reference. On rejection, the submitter releases the
provisional reference itself. On acceptance, the submitter never
releases the provisional reference, even if it observes a completed
job. This convention has an owner for each reference on every
path and does not need to infer ownership from a callback's timing.

Store the copied buffer in the job and destroy it when the last
reference ends, assuming completion is the last legal native read.
If a public future can refer to the job, give it its own reference
or move the submitter's reference into it. Do not give cancellation
authority to free the copy. To test the early-completion path,
run a library fixture that calls the callback before it returns
acceptance and then checks the submitting wrapper can still read
the completion record. Also reject submission without dispatch:
precisely the same native bytes must be reclaimed, by a different path.
